0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","1.8",{"topic":{"id":"1.8","title":"National Cyber Regulatory Framework","minutes":12,"summary":"See how government, regulators, incident responders, police, and sectoral authorities divide responsibility for cyberspace.","sections":[{"title":"Institutions and their mandates","body":["MeitY administers the IT Act and significant parts of digital policy. CERT-In performs national cyber incident coordination and issues directions under its statutory mandate. The National Critical Information Infrastructure Protection Centre performs functions relating to protected systems and critical sectors. The Data Protection Board is associated with the separate DPDP framework, subject to commencement and establishment notifications.","At the operational level, local police, state cyber cells, and the Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre support cybercrime response. I4C is a coordination framework, not a replacement for every legally competent police station. Sector regulators, such as the Reserve Bank of India, may impose additional security and incident-reporting requirements on regulated entities."]},{"title":"Different instruments, different force","body":["An Act creates primary legal obligations. Rules are delegated legislation made under an identified power. Directions and orders may implement specific statutory provisions. Policies, frameworks, advisories, and technical standards usually have different legal status. NIST CSF is a voluntary framework unless made applicable through a contract or another legal requirement; an ISO reference does not by itself create a criminal offence.","When responding to an incident, maintain an obligations register: instrument, issuing authority, applicable entity, trigger, deadline, recipient, and evidence of compliance. CERT-In’s 2022 directions include a six-hour reporting requirement for covered incidents, while other laws and regulators may impose additional duties. Do not assume that every organization or event has the same reporting clock."]},{"title":"Coordination without losing accountability","body":["A good response plan assigns legal authority, technical containment, evidence preservation, communications, and victim support. These roles can overlap but should not be left ambiguous. Record who approved each intrusive action and who received each data copy. A service provider’s fraud team and a criminal investigator need different information and different assurances.","The National Cyber Security Policy, 2013 articulated objectives such as workforce development, information sharing, and a secure computing environment. It should be studied as policy, alongside but not confused with later binding rules. For current operational requirements, consult the responsible institution’s latest official notification rather than relying on a policy’s aspirations."]}],"practice":{"title":"The first coordination call","scenario":"A payment company detects account takeover affecting customers in several states.","steps":["List internal incident response, CERT-In, sector regulator, police, and customer-support roles.","Identify which reporting obligations are actually triggered.","Create one shared incident identifier without circulating unnecessary personal data.","Define the next update time and a decision log owner."],"debrief":"Coordination succeeds when legal deadlines, technical decisions, and ownership are explicit. A long recipient list is not a response plan."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","dpdp","policy-2013"],"sources":["cert","i4c","policy2013","nist-csf","dpdp"],"unit":1,"objectives":["Explain institutions and their mandates and apply it to an investigation.","Explain different instruments, different force and apply it to an investigation.","Explain coordination without losing accountability and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-1.8","prompt":"Which statement about NIST CSF is most accurate?","options":["It is an Indian penal statute.","It automatically replaces CERT-In directions.","It is a framework whose legal effect depends on the context of adoption.","It certifies every tool as forensically sound."],"answer":2,"explanation":"NIST CSF organizes cybersecurity risk management; it is not a criminal code or automatic product certification.","unit":1}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
